faqs:
  - q: "How much corpus do I need to retire in India?"
    a: >-
      It depends on your expenses, not on a round number. A household spending ₹50,000 a month today and retiring in 30 years needs roughly ₹7.7 crore at 6% inflation to fund a 25-year retirement — because those expenses will have grown to about ₹2.87 lakh a month by then. The honest answer always starts from your own spending, which is what this calculator does.
  - q: "What is the difference between this and the FIRE calculator?"
    a: >-
      The FIRE calculator sizes a corpus meant to last indefinitely using a safe withdrawal rate, which suits early retirement at 40 or 45. This calculator models a conventional retirement with a defined end: it draws the corpus down over your remaining years, so it can assume a shorter horizon and typically produces a smaller target.
  - q: "What return should I assume after retirement?"
    a: >-
      Lower than before it. Retirees usually shift toward debt, so 7–8% is a common post-retirement assumption against 11–12% during accumulation. What matters is the gap between that return and inflation: if you earn 7% while inflation runs 6%, your real return is only about 0.9%, and the corpus has to be large enough to cover nearly the whole withdrawal itself.
  - q: "Does EPF and NPS count towards this?"
    a: >-
      Yes. Enter your current EPF, NPS, PPF and mutual fund balances together as existing savings — the calculator grows them at your expected return and subtracts the result from the target, so the monthly SIP it shows is only the additional amount you need. Remember that NPS requires at least 40% of the maturity corpus to buy an annuity.
  - q: "What if I retire earlier than 60?"
    a: >-
      The corpus requirement rises sharply from both directions: you have fewer years to accumulate and more years to fund. Retiring at 50 instead of 60 roughly triples the monthly saving required. If you are planning that, use the FIRE calculator as well, since a 35-year retirement is better sized with a withdrawal rate than a fixed end date.
  - q: "Should I include my house in the corpus?"
    a: >-
      Only if you genuinely intend to sell or rent it. A self-occupied home produces no income and still costs maintenance, insurance and property tax, so most planners exclude it from the retirement corpus and count only financial assets. Rental income, if any, can be treated as a reduction in your expenses instead.
