<article class="article">
  <h2>How the corpus is calculated</h2>
  <p>Retirement planning is two problems joined at one date. Before that date you are accumulating; after it you are withdrawing from a pot that still earns a return while inflation keeps raising what you withdraw. This calculator solves both:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Inflate your expenses</strong> to your retirement year: current expenses × (1 + inflation)<sup>years to retirement</sup>.</li>
    <li><strong>Size the corpus</strong> as the present value of a rising stream of withdrawals lasting your full retirement, discounted at your post-retirement return.</li>
    <li><strong>Grow what you already have</strong> at your pre-retirement return and subtract it.</li>
    <li><strong>Convert the shortfall</strong> into the monthly SIP that closes it by your retirement date.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Step two uses the <strong>real return</strong> — the part of your post-retirement return that beats inflation:</p>
  <div class="formula">real return = (1 + post-retirement return) ÷ (1 + inflation) − 1</div>
  <p>This is the number that decides everything after you stop working. At a 7% return and 6% inflation the real return is only about 0.94%, so a 25-year retirement needs a corpus of about 22 times the first year's expenses. Push inflation to 7% against the same 7% return and the real return falls to zero — the corpus then has to be a full 25 times, because the portfolio contributes nothing beyond keeping pace.</p>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>You are 30, plan to retire at 60 and to fund life until 85. You spend ₹50,000 a month today, assume 6% inflation, 12% returns while working and 7% after, and already hold ₹5,00,000.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your ₹50,000 of monthly spending becomes about <strong>₹2.87 lakh a month</strong> by the time you are 60.</li>
    <li>Funding that for 25 years needs a corpus of roughly <strong>₹7.7 crore</strong>.</li>
    <li>Your existing ₹5 lakh grows to about ₹1.5 crore over 30 years at 12%, leaving about ₹6.2 crore to find.</li>
    <li>That shortfall needs a SIP of roughly <strong>₹17,600 a month</strong>, starting now.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The striking part is how much the ₹5 lakh already invested does over thirty years — it alone covers nearly a fifth of the target. Start the same plan at 40 instead of 30, or retire at 50 rather than 60, and the required SIP multiplies several times over.</p>

  <h2>Getting the inputs right</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Use real expenses, not take-home pay.</strong> Include rent or maintenance, insurance, travel and the irregular costs that never appear in a monthly budget. Exclude things that will have stopped by retirement — EMIs that will be repaid, children's education, and your own retirement saving.</li>
    <li><strong>Plan to a realistic age.</strong> Indian life expectancy averages are not the right input; you are planning for the chance of living longer than average. Planning to 85 or 90 is prudent, and running out at 82 is a far worse error than leaving money behind.</li>
    <li><strong>Healthcare deserves its own provision.</strong> Medical inflation runs well above general inflation, and a retiree without strong health cover can lose years of corpus to a single episode. Treat comprehensive health insurance as a prerequisite, not a line item.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not assume equity returns through retirement.</strong> A retiree drawing an income cannot ride out a long drawdown, which is why the post-retirement return defaults to 7% here rather than 12%.</li>
    <li><strong>Revisit every year.</strong> Compare actual inflation and portfolio value against the plan. Corrections made annually are small; corrections made after a decade rarely are.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>After you retire</h2>
  <p>Building the corpus is only half the job — drawing it down without exhausting it is the other half. A systematic withdrawal plan is the usual mechanism in India, and the <a href="/swp-calculator/">SWP calculator</a> shows how long a given corpus survives at a chosen monthly withdrawal. If you are aiming to stop work well before 60, the <a href="/fire-calculator/">FIRE calculator</a> sizes an open-ended corpus instead of one with an end date.</p>
</article>

<script>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
  function recalc() {
    var age = Math.round(CM.val('age'));
    var rage = Math.round(CM.val('rage'));
    var life = Math.round(CM.val('life'));
    var exp = CM.val('exp');
    var inf = CM.val('inf') / 100;
    var rpre = CM.val('rpre') / 100;
    var rpost = CM.val('rpost') / 100;
    var have = CM.val('have');

    var n = rage - age;          // years accumulating
    var m = life - rage;         // years in retirement
    if (n <= 0 || m <= 0) {
      CM.set('out-corpus', '—');
      CM.set('out-corpus-sub', n <= 0 ? 'Retirement age must be above your current age' : 'Plan-until age must be above retirement age');
      ['out-exp', 'out-have', 'out-gap', 'out-sip'].forEach(function (id) { CM.set(id, '—'); });
      return;
    }

    // expenses at retirement
    var annualNow = exp * 12;
    var annualAtRet = annualNow * Math.pow(1 + inf, n);

    // corpus = PV of an inflation-rising withdrawal stream, discounted at post-retirement return
    var rr = (1 + rpost) / (1 + inf) - 1;   // real return
    var corpus;
    if (Math.abs(rr) < 1e-9) {
      corpus = annualAtRet * m;
    } else {
      corpus = annualAtRet * (1 - Math.pow(1 + rr, -m)) / rr * (1 + rr); // withdrawals at start of year
    }

    var haveFv = have * Math.pow(1 + rpre, n);
    var gap = Math.max(0, corpus - haveFv);

    var i = rpre / 12, months = n * 12;
    var sip = i === 0 ? gap / months : gap / (((Math.pow(1 + i, months) - 1) / i) * (1 + i));

    CM.set('out-corpus', CM.inr(corpus));
    var w = CM.words(corpus);
    CM.set('out-corpus-sub', (w ? 'about ' + w + ' · ' : '') + 'funds ' + m + ' years');
    CM.set('out-exp', CM.inr(annualAtRet / 12));
    CM.set('out-exp-sub', 'at age ' + rage + ', today ' + CM.inr(exp));
    CM.set('out-have', CM.inr(haveFv));
    CM.set('out-gap', CM.inr(gap));
    CM.set('out-sip', gap <= 0 ? 'On track ✓' : CM.inr(sip));
    CM.set('out-sip-sub', gap <= 0 ? 'existing savings already cover it' : 'for ' + n + ' years, at ' + CM.num(CM.val('rpre'), 1) + '%');

    // two-phase chart: accumulate to retirement, then draw down
    var labels = [], data = [];
    var bal = have;
    for (var y = 0; y <= n; y++) {
      labels.push('Age ' + (age + y));
      data.push(bal);
      if (y < n) bal = bal * (1 + rpre) + sip * 12 * (1 + rpre);
    }
    var pot = corpus, wd = annualAtRet;
    for (var k = 1; k <= m; k++) {
      pot = (pot - wd) * (1 + rpost);
      wd = wd * (1 + inf);
      labels.push('Age ' + (rage + k));
      data.push(Math.max(0, pot));
    }
    CM.lineChart('chart-life', labels, [{ label: 'Corpus', data: data, role: '--s1', fill: true }]);
  }
  CM.bind(['age', 'rage', 'life', 'exp', 'inf', 'rpre', 'rpost', 'have'], recalc);
});
</script>
